this is a poster presentation for a  project done in collaboration between  the gucci memorial institute for medical  research  and pine biotech my name is ilya brodsky  and i am working together with our team  at pine biotech dr mohit mazumdar and dr  harpreet or  as well as our collaborators at  university of ghana dr anita kansa  and dr benedict mensah our poster is on  plasmodium falciparum  apical membrane antigen robbery neck  protein 2 binding  which is a critical interaction for  plasmodium foscipromerozite invasion  of human host red blood cells several  proposed malaria vaccine candidates  designed to prevent such binding  and limit merozoite entry have been  studied with variable success  at in vitro and early clinical trial  stages  by performing haplotype network analysis  of genomic sequencing data from sites  with variable infection rates  comparing our data with malaria gen  repository from another region in ghana  and mapping of genomic variants onto the  protein structures  we arrive at preliminary conclusions  about local variation  and immunogenic epitope affecting  variants for consideration  in this poster we share preliminary  findings that suggest  that the genomic data can be used to  develop a local strategy for efficacious  vaccine design  against plasmodium falciparum blood  stage infection in ghana  and surrounding regions ma1 run 2  binding is a critical interaction for  p-falciparum  merizoit invasion of host red blood  cells which causes clinical  manifestation of malaria  several proposed malaria vaccine  candidates designed to prevent such  binding  and limit merozoite entry have been  studied with variable success  at in vitro and early clinical trial  stages  one key observation about vaccine  efficacy based on these proteins is the  genetic diversity  of ma1 in parasite populations  associated with disease burden  transmission rates and various  environmental factors  our team here analyzes ama1 genomic data  from the goron  cape coast in ghana the two towns are  240 kilometers apart  and represent parasite populations in  the forest and coastal savannah  ecological regions of ghana  with variable transmission rates in this  project we wanted to  understand how ama1 protein in these  populations differs from the 3d7 strain  of p falciparum  that is considered to be prevalent in  sub-saharan africa and was used for the  vaccine being considered  in this region two identify prevalent  snips  and specific mutation signatures for  each population  and compare those with data from other  regions in ghana  three see whether snips in  immunodominant domains are affected by  variable immune pressures  by exploring b-cell and t-cell epitopes  and four  ask the question about potential gene  flow between parasites in these two  towns  located in different ecological regions  our analysis workflow combines  processing of raw data  alignment of consensus sequences for the  reference 3d7 strain  haplotype analysis to determine  population structure and variability  followed by identification of haplotypes  specific for each population  and combinations of snips in haplotypes  that are also specific to these  populations  the analysis was conducted using new  data collected from the goron cape coast  in ghana during the peak malaria  transmission seasons  over a four year period 150 sequences  from the goro  and 181 from cape coast were obtained by  molecular inversion probe capture and  deep sequencing on the aluminum ic  platform  raw sequencing reads were aligned to the  p4 ciprim reference genomes trained 3d7  using mip wrangler and seek deep  downstream analysis of synonymous and  non-synonymous  variants was conducted using eugene r  scripts and the t by info platform  to determine genetic diversity within  and between both populations we  performed haplotype analysis based on  the full sequence of ama1  as well as a ma1 domain 1 where the  binding site with ron 2 is located  to calculate a haplotype distribution we  used pigas  and ape are packages haplotype networks  were generated using the templeton  crandall and sting tcs method  and filtering haplotypes for frequency  of occurrence  haplotypes were further analyzed for  specificity of mutations to each  location  and their impact on protein structure to  explore the immune pressures on  population structures at both locations  we explored genomic variation and  predicted b-cell and t-cell epitopes  b-cell epitopes were predicted using the  bepi pred 2.0 method  and tepee tool pipeline for  computational prediction of t-cell  epitope candidates  on the iedb analysis resource server the  predicted peptide structures were mapped  onto the ama1 protein structure  3zwz for downstream analysis  analysis of genomic variation in  collected samples reveals  that the ama-1 in cape coast and vigoro  have different intra-population  diversity and many nucleotide  differences from the 3d7 laboratory  strain being used as the dominant strain  in people's eprom population in  sub-saharan africa  to understand the impact of this  variation on protein-coding  immunodominant regions  we performed analysis of non-synonymous  mutations in a critical  functional domain that has the potential  impact on immune evasion  first we considered the full length of  ama1  overall we identified common variants in  102 positions  in the 1767 nucleotide  long ama1 gene which is about six  percent  we identified several positions with  higher frequency of variance including  physicians 116  523 845 876  988 and 995. next we compared prevalence  of combinations of mutations using  haplotype  analysis using the tcs method we  conducted haplotype analysis which  identified 193 total haplotypes  from 331 samples from the goro and cape  coast  haplotype 1 is identical to people 37  reference  haplotype 1 was found in only 6 of the  samples  2 in vigoro and 4 in cape coast that is  less than 2 percent of the full  population  mutations at 6 positions were found to  be common  among most of the haplotypes g116a  t523g t845a  g876a c988t995a  four mutations are more prevalent in  cape coast than vigoro  and one mutation is more prevalent in  decoro  than cape coast then we wanted to  understand differences between parasite  populations in these regions of variable  transmission  we found that seventy percent of the  haplotypes had significant variation  over 26 variants or more than one  percent  and thirty percent had minor variation  from the reference p-falciprom3d7 strain  which is between one and twenty-six  variants of these observed haplotypes 62  occurred at least twice  and were used to construct a network  which shows haplotype frequency  distribution and divergence into  specific groups  each haplotype corresponds to a sequence  which can be found in both population or  belong to a specific population only  most haplotypes are more prevalent in  cape coast  next we filtered 42 key haplotypes out  of a total of 193 haplotypes with  frequency  difference of at least two between both  populations  out of these 42 19 haplotypes are unique  for cape coast  15 unique for bagoro and seven and one  significantly more prevalent in cape  coast and vigorous respectively  constructed network shows no significant  separation between the two locations  with most prevalent haplotypes shared  between populations  the ama1 protein consists of three  domains  domains one and two are strongly  conserved across the  epic complex of phylum suggesting a  concerned function for these domains  whereas domain 3 is divergent on the  other hand  within the plasmodium species  polymorphisms are concentrated in domain  1 and to a lesser extent domain 2.  domain 1  contains a hydrophobic groove where  binding with ron 2 has to take place for  parasite invasion of red blood cells  previous literature reports that the  population structure of ama1  alleles is reflective of diversifying  selection under the pressure of the host  immune system  we analyze haplotype diversity by  considering only  ama1 domain 1 nucleotide positions 445  to 906  haplotype analysis yielded 93  hamplotypes  27 haplotypes that are specific to the  goro  30 specific to cape coast and 36 shared  by both populations  among the 27 haplotypes that are  reasonably different between  these populations in terms of frequency  difference  more than two between populations had  six haplotypes that are specific to cape  coast and  seven that are specific to the goro 14  haplotypes that were shared  had 10 that are more prevalent in cape  coast  with a difference of at least two and  four in the goro  for domain one haplotypes we observed  that mutations t523g  and g876a are present in all haplotypes  except haplotype 1 which is identical to  the reference  mutations at 6 positions g116a  t523g t845a  g876a c988t  t995a were found to be common among most  of the haplotypes  four mutations t566c a729t  c848t a163c  are more prevalent in cape coast than  vigoro and one mutation g106a  is more prevalent in vigoro than cape  coast  next we wanted to understand how ama1  residue conservation varies between  populations  we analyzed ama1 conservation using the  al2co method  in ucsf chimera by calculation of  positional conservation in a protein  sequence alignment  based on entropy this entropy based  conservation analysis revealed variable  positions specific to the goro with high  variability in vigoro  and conserved in cape coast for begoro  high variability positions can be seen  in red  these regions were more variable in cape  coast cape coast high variability  positions can be seen in blue  these regions were more variable in cape  coast  we then looked at where these positions  are located based on ma1 domain 1.  we found that region 5 a position 221 to  224  and 3 a positions 167 to 170  are more variable in the goro and  conserved in cape coast  these locations are within the  hydrophobic region of ama1 round 2  binding which is essential for merozoite  entry into the red blood cell and is a  target for many considered anti-malarial  drugs conversely region 4 a185 to 188  is more variable in cape coast while it  is conserved in degoro  this region is likely important for  binding as well since it is a part of  the loop covering the hydrophobic  binding region  next we assessed how the haplotype  variants in ama1  might reflect immune pressure specific  to each region  and how these variants might affect  b-cell and t-cell recognition  by mapping identified variants on  predicted t-cell and b-cell epitopes  six variants were identified to have an  effect on b-cell epitopes  of which two l-189p and  s-283-l were more prevalent in cape  coast affecting b-cell epitopes  two mutations were unique and more  specific to the cape coast population  others were common polymorphic sites  prevalent in haplotypes for both  populations  interestingly one of the longest b-cell  epitopes  aa163 to 212 was determined to be  significantly more variable in bogoro  and conserved than cape coast as  described previously  varian i-332n which is common in both  populations  is found in one of the predicted b-cell  epitopes with a blossom score  of -3 analysis of predicted t-cell  epitopes was filtered for score above  0.9  one variant found in the predicted  epitopes is y175d  which was common for both sites and is  likely to affect t cell recognition by  its impact on mhc class 1 molecule  binding specificity  y to d blossom 62 score of minus 3.  discussion using ama1 sequence data  generated from mip capture and targeted  deep sequencing of 181 and 150 clinical  parasite isolates  from cape coast in the coastal savannah  region  and begoro in the forest region of ghana  we observed extensive nucleotide  variation  in the ama 1 genomic sequences there  were 193 haplotypes of which 70 percent  had over 26 variants this high genetic  diversity is known to occur in high  transmission areas  where infected individuals usually  harbor more polyclonal infections  compared to those living in low  transmission areas  where infections are more often  monoclonal  we found that circulating ama1  haplotypes in the cape coast  population were more diverse in  distribution and uniqueness  in both the full gene and the  immunodominant domain one  despite this high level of genetic  diversity including  multiple non-synonymous nucleotide  substitutions  population structure shows that there is  a shared gene pool between the two sites  as indicated in previous studies the  human population is likely to facilitate  gene flow among people supreme parasites  despite the ecological and  epidemiological diversity between the  two sites the human movement factor is a  significant  consideration with implications for the  spread of any emerging vaccine resistant  parasite  of significance is the low frequency of  3d7  ama1 reference haplotype in the two  ghanian sites  with implications for vaccine efficacy  several amino acid changes in domain 1  occurred in predicted b-cell epitopes  and may affect both naturally acquired  and vaccine-induced ma1 immunity in  addition  t-cell epitope variant y-175d  circulating on both sides is likely to  affect mhc  class 1 binding specificity with  implications for long-term vaccine  efficacy  novel sites mapped on the  three-dimensional structure of  ama1 showed that these regions were  located at exposed protrusions and are  unique to the two ghanian sites  potentially these mutations could be  affecting the binding between the two  proteins in ghana  in contrast to literature findings that  common mutations like these  do not affect binding affinity this  could be due to the fact that the  overall pocket properties are not  changed and ron 2 also has variation to  adapt  to this new confirmation one limitation  to this observation is that we did not  sequence the wrong to gene  in the same sites we are planning  additional studies to explore the impact  of found mutations on potential binding  and restrictions to propose drug  candidates aimed at this hydrophobic  site  we believe that the results highlight  important considerations and may provide  significant value to the design of a  malaria vaccine  based on this antigen and offer insights  into the efficacy of ama1 vaccines in  ghana  further analysis on molecular dynamics  of the major circulating ama1 variants  and the wrong two variants in ghana  will shed more light on the effect of  the extensive genetic diversity  on the red blood cell parasite invasion  and vaccine efficacy for the formulation  of more effective vaccines importantly  our study also demonstrates the utility  of in-depth bioinformatic analysis for  practical public health concerns  in this region

